---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../data/tools';

const tool = toolBySlug['small-business-loan-calculator'];
const breadcrumbItems = [{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: tool.title }];
const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "Small Business Loans: Rates, Terms & Free Calculator",
    "description": "Understand small business loan rates, terms, and qualification requirements. Calculate your EMI, total interest, amortization schedule, and debt-to-income ratio.",
    "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "FreeOnlineToolkit" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "FreeOnlineToolkit" },
    "datePublished": "2025-01-01",
    "dateModified": "2025-01-01",
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "How to Calculate Small Business Loan Payments",
    "description": "Calculate your small business loan EMI, total interest, and debt-to-income ratio using loan amount, interest rate, and term.",
    "step": [
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Enter the total loan amount you wish to borrow" },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Input the annual interest rate as a percentage" },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Select the loan term in months or years" },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Optionally enter your monthly revenue to calculate debt-to-income ratio" },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Click Calculate to see your monthly payment and amortization schedule" },
    ],
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout title="Small Business Loans: Rates, Terms & Free Calculator — FreeOnlineToolkit" description="Understand small business loan rates, terms, and requirements. Calculate your EMI, total interest, and debt-to-income ratio." breadcrumbItems={breadcrumbItems} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <fragment slot="heading"><h1>Small Business Loans: Rates, Terms & Free Calculator</h1></fragment>
  <fragment slot="intro">
    <div class="key-takeaways">
      <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Small business loan rates range from <strong>6% (bank loans) to 30%+ (online lenders)</strong> — your credit score and business history determine where you fall</li>
        <li>Lenders look at your <strong>debt-to-income (DTI) ratio</strong> — keep it below 35% for the best approval odds and rates</li>
        <li>SBA loans offer the <strong>best balance of rate and accessibility</strong>, with government backing that reduces lender risk</li>
        <li>Longer loan terms mean <strong>lower monthly payments but higher total interest</strong> — the right term balances cash flow with total cost</li>
        <li><strong>Prepayment penalties</strong> of 1-3% are common on business loans — always check before paying early</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>Securing the right financing is one of the most critical decisions a small business owner faces. Whether you're launching a startup, expanding operations, purchasing equipment, or managing cash flow gaps, a small business loan can provide the capital you need to grow. However, the world of business lending is complex — rates, terms, qualification requirements, and repayment structures vary dramatically between lenders, and choosing the wrong loan can cost tens of thousands of dollars in unnecessary interest.</p>

    <p>Small business loans come in several forms. <strong>Term loans</strong> provide a lump sum upfront that you repay with interest over a fixed period, typically 1 to 10 years. <strong>SBA loans</strong> are partially guaranteed by the Small Business Administration, which reduces risk for lenders and allows them to offer competitive rates (typically 7-13%) even to businesses that might not qualify for traditional bank loans. <strong>Business lines of credit</strong> offer flexible borrowing up to a limit — you draw what you need and pay interest only on what you use. <strong>Equipment financing</strong> uses the equipment itself as collateral, and <strong>invoice factoring</strong> lets you sell unpaid invoices for immediate cash.</p>

    <p>The interest rate you're offered depends on several factors: your personal and business credit scores, time in business (lenders prefer 2+ years), annual revenue, existing debt, and the loan type. Traditional bank loans offer the lowest rates (5-9%) but have strict qualification requirements. Online lenders provide fast funding and easier qualification but charge significantly higher rates (15-40%). SBA loans sit in the middle, offering competitive rates with more paperwork and longer processing times. Understanding these tradeoffs is essential for choosing the right financing for your situation.</p>

    <p>Before applying, calculate your <strong>debt-to-income ratio</strong> (monthly debt payments divided by monthly revenue). Most lenders want to see a DTI below 35-50%. Also consider the <strong>total cost of the loan</strong>, not just the monthly payment. A 5-year loan at 8.5% on $100,000 results in about $22,800 in total interest — meaning you repay nearly $123,000 in total. The amortization schedule below breaks this down year by year, showing how much of each payment goes to principal versus interest.</p>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="calculator">
    <div class="calc-card">
      <h2>Business Loan Calculator</h2>
      <div class="form-group">
        <label for="loanAmount">Loan Amount ($)</label>
        <div class="input-wrap">
          <span class="prefix">$</span>
          <input type="number" id="loanAmount" class="prefixed" value="100000" min="0" step="1000">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="form-row">
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="interestRate">Annual Interest Rate (%)</label>
          <input type="number" id="interestRate" value="8.5" min="0" step="0.1">
        </div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="loanTerm">Loan Term</label>
          <input type="number" id="loanTerm" value="5" min="1" max="30">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="form-row">
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="termUnit">Term Unit</label>
          <select id="termUnit">
            <option value="years" selected>Years</option>
            <option value="months">Months</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="monthlyRevenue">Monthly Revenue ($) <span class="optional">optional</span></label>
          <div class="input-wrap">
            <span class="prefix">$</span>
            <input type="number" id="monthlyRevenue" class="prefixed" value="0" min="0" step="1000">
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="btn-group">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" id="calcBtn" data-i18n="common.calculate">Calculate</button>
        <button class="btn btn-secondary" id="resetBtn" data-i18n="common.reset">Reset</button>
      </div>

      <div class="result-box" id="bizLoanResult">
        <div class="result-value" id="bizMonthlyPayment">$0.00</div>
        <div class="result-label">Monthly Payment (EMI)</div>
        <div class="result-details">
          <div><span class="label">Total Interest</span><span class="value" id="bizTotalInterest">$0</span></div>
          <div><span class="label">Total Repayment</span><span class="value" id="bizTotalRepayment">$0</span></div>
          <div><span class="label">Number of Payments</span><span class="value" id="bizNumPayments">0</span></div>
          <div><span class="label">Debt-to-Income Ratio</span><span class="value" id="bizDTI">--</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section" id="bizAmortSection" style="display:none;">
      <h2>Yearly Amortization Schedule</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="amort-table" id="bizAmortTable">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Year</th><th>Beginning Balance</th><th>Total Paid</th><th>Total Interest</th><th>Ending Balance</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody id="bizAmortBody"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="content">
    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>What This Calculator Shows</h2>
      <p>This business loan calculator shows your <strong>monthly EMI payment</strong>, <strong>total interest</strong> over the loan term, <strong>total repayment amount</strong>, and the <strong>number of payments</strong>. If you enter your monthly revenue, it also calculates your <strong>debt-to-income (DTI) ratio</strong> — a key metric lenders use to evaluate your loan application. The yearly amortization schedule shows how each year's payments break down between principal and interest.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>How to Use the Small Business Loan Calculator</h2>
      <p>Using this business loan calculator is straightforward. Start by entering the <strong>loan amount</strong> you plan to borrow for your business. Then input the <strong>annual interest rate</strong> your lender has quoted. Business loan rates typically range from 6% to 30% depending on your creditworthiness, business history, and loan type.</p>
      <p>Choose your <strong>loan term</strong> in years or months &mdash; most small business loans range from 1 to 10 years. You can switch between years and months using the dropdown. If you enter your <strong>monthly revenue</strong>, the calculator will also show your debt-to-income (DTI) ratio, which lenders use to assess your ability to repay.</p>
      <p>Click "Calculate" and you'll see your monthly EMI payment, total interest, and total repayment amount. The yearly amortization schedule breaks down how much of each year's payments go toward principal versus interest.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Business Loan EMI Formula</h2>
      <p>The monthly payment is calculated using the standard amortization formula:</p>
      <div class="formula-box">EMI = P &times; [r(1+r)^n] / [(1+r)^n &minus; 1]</div>
      <p>Where: <strong>EMI</strong> = monthly payment, <strong>P</strong> = loan principal, <strong>r</strong> = monthly interest rate (annual rate / 12), and <strong>n</strong> = total number of monthly payments.</p>
      <p>The <strong>debt-to-income ratio</strong> is calculated as: DTI = (Monthly Payment / Monthly Revenue) &times; 100.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Next Steps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Check your credit score</strong> before applying — both personal and business scores affect your rate and approval odds</li>
        <li><strong>Compare at least 3-5 lenders</strong> including banks, credit unions, SBA lenders, and online platforms to find the best terms</li>
        <li><strong>Gather your documents</strong> — tax returns, financial statements, business plan, and bank statements are typically required</li>
        <li><strong>Consider the total cost</strong>, not just the monthly payment — a longer term lowers payments but increases total interest paid</li>
        <li><strong>Negotiate terms</strong> — rates and fees are often negotiable, especially with banks and credit unions where you have an existing relationship</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <FAQ items={[
      { question: "What types of small business loans are available?", answer: "Common types include term loans (lump sum repaid over a fixed period), SBA loans (government-backed with competitive rates), business lines of credit (flexible borrowing up to a limit), and equipment financing. Each has different rates, terms, and qualification requirements." },
      { question: "What is a good interest rate for a small business loan?", answer: "Rates vary widely based on your credit score, business revenue, time in business, and loan type. SBA loans typically range from 7-10%, traditional bank loans from 6-12%, and online lenders from 10-30%. Compare multiple offers to find the best rate." },
      { question: "How is my debt-to-income ratio calculated for a business loan?", answer: "Lenders calculate DTI by dividing your total monthly debt payments (including this loan) by your monthly business revenue. A DTI below 35% is considered healthy, 36-50% may require explanation, and above 50% often leads to loan denial." },
      { question: "Can I pay off my business loan early?", answer: "Many business loans allow early repayment, but some charge prepayment penalties. Check your loan agreement before making extra payments. Paying early saves on interest but may come with a fee (typically 1-3% of the remaining balance)." },
    ]} />
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="sidebar">
    <RelatedTools currentSlug="small-business-loan-calculator" category="business-finance" />
  </fragment>
</ToolLayout>

<script>
import { getNum, formatCurrency, showResult, hideResult } from '../utils/calculator-utils';

function calcBizLoan() {
  const P = getNum('loanAmount');
  const annualRate = getNum('interestRate');
  let term = getNum('loanTerm');
  const unit = (document.getElementById('termUnit') as HTMLSelectElement).value;
  const revenue = getNum('monthlyRevenue') || 0;

  if (!P || !annualRate || !term) return;
  if (unit === 'years') term = term * 12;

  const r = annualRate / 100 / 12;
  const n = term;
  const M = P * (r * Math.pow(1 + r, n)) / (Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1);
  const totalRepayment = M * n;
  const totalInterest = totalRepayment - P;

  document.getElementById('bizMonthlyPayment')!.textContent = formatCurrency(M);
  document.getElementById('bizTotalInterest')!.textContent = formatCurrency(totalInterest);
  document.getElementById('bizTotalRepayment')!.textContent = formatCurrency(totalRepayment);
  document.getElementById('bizNumPayments')!.textContent = n.toString();

  if (revenue > 0) {
    const dti = (M / revenue) * 100;
    document.getElementById('bizDTI')!.textContent = dti.toFixed(1) + '%';
  } else {
    document.getElementById('bizDTI')!.textContent = '--';
  }

  showResult('bizLoanResult');

  const years = Math.ceil(n / 12);
  let balance = P;
  const yearlyBody = document.getElementById('bizAmortBody')!;
  yearlyBody.innerHTML = '';

  for (let y = 1; y <= years; y++) {
    const startBal = balance;
    let yearlyTotalPaid = 0;
    let yearlyTotalInterest = 0;
    const monthsInYear = (y === years && n % 12 !== 0) ? n % 12 : 12;

    for (let m = 0; m < monthsInYear; m++) {
      const interestPmt = balance * r;
      const principalPmt = M - interestPmt;
      balance = balance - principalPmt;
      yearlyTotalPaid += M;
      yearlyTotalInterest += interestPmt;
    }

    if (balance < 0) balance = 0;

    const row = document.createElement('tr');
    row.innerHTML = '<td>' + y + '</td><td>' + formatCurrency(startBal) + '</td><td>' + formatCurrency(yearlyTotalPaid) + '</td><td>' + formatCurrency(yearlyTotalInterest) + '</td><td>' + formatCurrency(balance) + '</td>';
    yearlyBody.appendChild(row);
  }

  document.getElementById('bizAmortSection')!.style.display = 'block';
}

function resetBizLoan() {
  (document.getElementById('loanAmount') as HTMLInputElement).value = '100000';
  (document.getElementById('interestRate') as HTMLInputElement).value = '8.5';
  (document.getElementById('loanTerm') as HTMLInputElement).value = '5';
  (document.getElementById('termUnit') as HTMLSelectElement).value = 'years';
  (document.getElementById('monthlyRevenue') as HTMLInputElement).value = '0';
  document.getElementById('bizAmortSection')!.style.display = 'none';
  hideResult('bizLoanResult');
}

document.getElementById('calcBtn')!.addEventListener('click', calcBizLoan);
document.getElementById('resetBtn')!.addEventListener('click', resetBizLoan);
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', calcBizLoan);
</script>
